  {
    "id": "what",
    "title": "Clauses under stress",
    "labels": [
      "Attention",
      "Ecological budgets",
      "Legitimate abundance"
    ],
    "lines": [
      "Later clauses protect attention, privacy, ecological budgets, reproductive freedom and room for new generations.",
      "The final chapters ask who pays, who enforces, and why powerful actors would comply.",
      "Its test is clear: abundance is legitimate when it reduces dependency, not when it makes dependency comfortable.",
      "Begin with the Introduction, where the book sets out the political offer of an automated order."
    ],
    "emotionalPlan": {
      "arc": "Widening scope, then a probing challenge, a clear verdict, and a warm invitation to read.",
      "purpose": "Preview the clauses, state the book's test of legitimate abundance, and give a real starting point.",
      "states": [
        "expansive",
        "probing",
        "convinced",
        "inviting"
      ],
      "intensities": [
        0.5,
        0.7,
        0.6,
        0.3
      ],
      "voiceDirections": [
        "Flowing and open, letting the list breathe.",
        "Pointed, with a small pause before comply.",
        "Clear and weighted; contrast reduces with comfortable.",
        "Warm and unhurried, a direct invitation."
      ]
    },
